+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 global fetch() honour HTTPS_PROXY by default?
+
+**Answer: No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTPS_PROXY`, `HTTP_PROXY` and
+their lowercase forms unless you opt in. To opt in, set `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` or pass
+`--use-env-proxy`. With the opt-in, Node installs undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent` as the
+global dispatcher, and `fetch()` then tunnels through the proxy.
+
+Version checked: **Node v22.23.4** (`/opt/node22/bin/node`). Web access was not
+available in this session, so all evidence comes from that binary: its CLI help, the
+lib source compiled into it, and a local measurement. The online docs were not
+fetched (see Open questions).
+
+## Findings
+
+| # | Claim | Evidence | Kind |
+|---|-------|----------|------|
+| 1 | Out of the box, `fetch()` connects directly and never contacts the proxy in `HTTPS_PROXY`/`HTTP_PROXY`, uppercase or lowercase. | Probe below: the fake proxy received 0 requests and fetch failed with `ENOTFOUND` from a direct DNS lookup. | measured |
+| 2 | With `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` **or** `--use-env-proxy`, `fetch()` sends `CONNECT host:443` to the proxy. | Probe: the proxy logged `CONNECT proxy-probe.invalid:443 HTTP/1.1` in both cases. | measured |
+| 3 | The gate is the `--use-env-proxy` option, and `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` is normalised into it. Its default value is `false`. | `internal/process/pre_execution` lines 194–213 (`setupHttpProxy`): `if (!getOptionValue('--use-env-proxy')) return;`, with the comment "This normalized from both --use-env-proxy and NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY settings". `getOptionValue('--use-env-proxy')` returns `false` when neither is set. | read (source) + measured |
+| 4 | When enabled, and only if at least one of `HTTP_PROXY`/`HTTPS_PROXY`/`http_proxy`/`https_proxy` is set, Node calls `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())`. That is how `fetch()` picks up the proxy. | Same function, lines 199–206. | read (source) |
+| 5 | Node's own team chose not to enable this by default. They worry about conflicts with libraries that set the global dispatcher or monkey-patch the global agent. | Same function, lines 210–212: "TODO(joyeecheung): This is currently guarded with NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY and --use-env-proxy. Investigate whether it's possible to enable it by default without stepping on other existing libraries…" | read (source) |
+| 6 | The same opt-in also applies proxy env vars to `http`/`https` global agents. | `_http_agent` line 722 and `https` line 638: `proxyEnv: getOptionValue('--use-env-proxy') ? filterEnvForProxies(process.env) : undefined`. | read (source) |
+| 7 | The CLI documents the flag as "parse proxy settings from HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY/NO_PROXY environment variables and apply the setting in global HTTP/HTTPS clients". | `node --help` (v22.23.4). | read (CLI help) |
+| 8 | In v22.23.4 the feature prints a warning: `[UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental`. | Probe stderr. | measured |
+
+Source line numbers refer to the lib modules compiled into v22.23.4. I extracted them with
+`node --expose-internals -e "require('internal/test/binding').internalBinding('builtins').natives['internal/process/pre_execution']"`.
+The same files live upstream at `lib/internal/process/pre_execution.js`, `lib/_http_agent.js`
+and `lib/https.js` on the `v22.x` branch of github.com/nodejs/node.
+
+## Probe (measured)
+
+A local TCP server acts as the proxy. A child `node -e "fetch('https://proxy-probe.invalid/')"`
+starts with the proxy env vars already set. The `.invalid` TLD guarantees a direct
+connection fails at DNS, so no traffic leaves the machine.
+
+| Configuration | Proxy saw | fetch outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| `HTTPS_PROXY` + `HTTP_PROXY` set | nothing | `ENOTFOUND` (direct) |
+| `https_proxy` (lowercase) set | nothing | `ENOTFOUND` (direct) |
+| above + `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` | `CONNECT proxy-probe.invalid:443` | cancelled (proxy returned 502) |
+| above + `--use-env-proxy` | `CONNECT proxy-probe.invalid:443` | cancelled (proxy returned 502) |
+
+Script (re-runnable with `node probe.js`):
+
+```js
+const net = require('net');
+const { execFile } = require('child_process');
+const child = `fetch('https://proxy-probe.invalid/')
+  .then(r => console.log('status ' + r.status))
+  .catch(e => console.log('error: ' + (e.cause?.code || e.cause?.message || e.message)));`;
+let hits = [];
+const srv = net.createServer((s) => s.once('data', (d) => {
+  hits.push(d.toString().split('\r\n')[0]);
+  s.end('HTTP/1.1 502 Bad Gateway\r\nContent-Length: 0\r\n\r\n');
+}));
+const run = (label, extraEnv, args) => new Promise((resolve) => {
+  hits = [];
+  execFile(process.execPath, [...args, '-e', child],
+    { env: { PATH: process.env.PATH, ...extraEnv }, timeout: 20000 },
+    (err, out) => resolve({ label, fetchOutcome: out.trim(), proxyHits: [...hits] }));
+});
+srv.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', async () => {
+  const proxy = `http://127.0.0.1:${srv.address().port}`;
+  const base = { HTTPS_PROXY: proxy, HTTP_PROXY: proxy };
+  console.log(JSON.stringify([
+    await run('default', base, []),
+    await run('lowercase', { https_proxy: proxy }, []),
+    await run('NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1', { ...base, NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY: '1' }, []),
+    await run('--use-env-proxy', base, ['--use-env-proxy']),
+  ], null, 2));
+  srv.close();
+});
+```
+
+## Practical guidance (inferred)
+
+- To make `fetch()` use the proxy in Node 22, start Node with `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` or
+  `--use-env-proxy`. On builds without this option, set the dispatcher yourself:
+  `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())` from the `undici` npm package.
+- The env var form also works through `NODE_OPTIONS`-less launchers such as npm scripts and
+  Docker `ENV`, because Node reads it at startup.
+- If code sets its own global dispatcher after startup, or passes `dispatcher` per call,
+  it overrides this behaviour. This follows from `setGlobalDispatcher` semantics and was not measured.
+
+## Open questions
+
+- **First Node 22.x release that has the opt-in.** I could not check the docs' `added:`
+  metadata offline. It is present in v22.23.4. From memory, it reached 22.x around
+  v22.21.0, after landing in v24. Verify against
+  https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html#node_use_env_proxy1 before relying on
+  it for an older 22.x.
+- **`NO_PROXY` handling.** The CLI help names `NO_PROXY`, and undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent`
+  is expected to honour it. This was not measured here.
+- **Stability.** I did not confirm the feature's stability label in the published v22 docs.
+  The runtime warning says "experimental".
